---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ExampleCanvas from "../components/ExampleCanvas.astro";
import { examples, exampleHref } from "../examples";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const all = examples();
const basic = all.find((e) => e.category === "core" && e.name === "core_basic_window");
if (!basic) throw new Error("core_basic_window is missing from raylib's examples list");
const desktop = all.filter((e) => e.desktop.kind === "verified").length;
const browser = all.filter((e) => e.webVerified).length;
---

<Base title="raylib-zig">
  <h1>raylib, ported to Zig</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    raylib-zig is raylib 6.2 rewritten in Zig. It exports the same C API, so a C program written for raylib links
    against it unchanged. On top of that it has a Zig API in raylib's own style.
  </p>

  <p class="prose">
    For every upstream example that can run deterministically, the Zig library renders a final frame byte-identical to
    C raylib ({desktop} of {desktop}, on Linux, desktop GLFW, OpenGL 3.3). In the browser, {browser} examples are checked
    the same way so far. <a href={`${base}compatibility/`}>How that is checked</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Try it</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    This is raylib's first example, <a href={exampleHref(base, basic)}>basic window</a>, written with the Zig API and
    compiled to WebAssembly. What runs is the Zig port, not C raylib.
  </p>
  <ExampleCanvas example={basic} />

  <p class="prose">
    <a href={`${base}examples/`}>All {all.length} examples</a>, each with its Zig and C source side by side.
  </p>
</Base>
